WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
Two rules that do most of the work.

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:03.160
The shape rule.

3
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.048
The gradient of the loss with respect to a tensor has exactly that tensor’s shape.

4
00:00:08.328 --> 00:00:11.635
If W is 30 by 200, so is dW.

5
00:00:11.915 --> 00:00:23.669
This sounds trivial, and it’s the most useful debugging tool in the lecture: when you’re unsure whether a product needs a transpose or which axis to sum, the shapes usually leave only one option.

6
00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:25.656
Broadcast and sum are duals.

7
00:00:25.936 --> 00:00:42.832
If the forward pass replicates a value (adding a bias of shape (H) to every row of an (N, H) matrix, or subtracting a per-feature mean from every example), each copy is a separate use, and the backward pass must sum the gradients over the replicated axis.

8
00:00:43.112 --> 00:00:53.053
Conversely, if the forward pass sums over an axis (a row sum to normalize probabilities), the backward pass broadcasts the incoming gradient back along it.

9
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:57.429
This is the tensor form of lesson 1’s “the chain rule sums over paths.

10
00:00:57.709 --> 00:00:58.007
”.

11
00:00:58.287 --> 00:01:03.364
With those two rules plus each operation’s local derivative, the rest is bookkeeping.
